WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:05.250
Film Courage: Advice to first time, second
time filmmakers on seeking a distribution

00:00:05.250 --> 00:00:07.939
deal and then actually committing to it?

00:00:07.939 --> 00:00:14.000
Jay Silverman, filmmaker/photographer: It’s
a tough question for me because every time

00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:17.770
you make a deal you learn about new deals.

00:00:17.770 --> 00:00:23.720
I’ll give you an illustration, when we made
my first movie we didn’t have people knocking

00:00:23.720 --> 00:00:26.440
on my door to distribute my movie.

00:00:26.440 --> 00:00:32.180
We had sales reps I never knew what that was
but there are a lot of companies in L.A. and

00:00:32.180 --> 00:00:36.430
in Hollywood that are sales reps (the rep
movies) and they would take your movie to

00:00:36.430 --> 00:00:41.790
Netflix and they would take your movie to
Fox and they would make deals.

00:00:41.790 --> 00:00:43.970
I totally believe in that scenario.

00:00:43.970 --> 00:00:50.480
It never even occurred to me that my next
movie would be good enough for someone to

00:00:50.480 --> 00:00:52.190
want to distribute it.

00:00:52.190 --> 00:00:55.200
Of course, there is no money exchanged.

00:00:55.200 --> 00:01:01.590
You have to wait to get the movie distributed
and then eventually make your money back.

00:01:01.590 --> 00:01:06.300
If you’re lucky enough to hypothetically
wake up in the morning at Sundance and someone

00:01:06.300 --> 00:01:12.510
buys your movie to distribute it they might
say say we’ll give you X dollars not to

00:01:12.510 --> 00:01:17.420
mention that after advertising dollars are
spent we’ll give you 50 cents on the dollar

00:01:17.420 --> 00:01:20.280
you know whatever it might be.

00:01:20.280 --> 00:01:26.100
I did not have that luxury but I did have
the luxury this time of getting a reputable

00:01:26.100 --> 00:01:32.730
distribution company and taking it up a notch
so each time I learn more.

00:01:32.730 --> 00:01:39.790
But distribution and I’m saying this redundantly
because I said it before is an every-changing

00:01:39.790 --> 00:01:47.920
medium and I’m learning right as we speak
that filmmakers are expected to do things

00:01:47.920 --> 00:01:51.950
that have nothing to do with filmmaking and
that is if you really want to succeed with

00:01:51.950 --> 00:01:53.590
your movie and that is if you really want
to succeed with your movie social media has

00:01:53.590 --> 00:01:59.100
to be played, you have to market yourself,
you have to do interviews like this.

00:01:59.100 --> 00:02:05.910
I’m really grateful but I’m not a social
media guy but you have to learn how to do

00:02:05.910 --> 00:02:06.910
that.

00:02:06.910 --> 00:02:14.400
It’s fascinating because I have a number
of people on my Facebook that are filmmakers

00:02:14.400 --> 00:02:19.990
and every once in awhile I see a filmmaker
promoting their movie and I’m empathetic

00:02:19.990 --> 00:02:29.080
because it requires just an inordinate amount
of collective collaboration with the distributor

00:02:29.080 --> 00:02:36.000
to succeed because the distributor who picked
up my movie puts out four movies a month.

00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:37.430
That’s 48 movies a year.

00:02:37.430 --> 00:02:39.690
That’s a lot of movies for a small company.

00:02:39.690 --> 00:02:46.959
And one of the ones I passed on has 30 to
40 movies a month.

00:02:46.959 --> 00:02:51.770
And there in to the volume aspect so I thought
that the one that I picked was more desirable

00:02:51.770 --> 00:02:56.459
because they would give it more energy and
more time and I don’t have a regret for

00:02:56.459 --> 00:02:57.459
that.

00:02:57.459 --> 00:03:01.300
But here again speaking from first time experience.

00:03:01.300 --> 00:03:07.730
I met a gentleman just last week who is doing
our social media and he’s also a director.

00:03:07.730 --> 00:03:17.420
Smart, young guy and 
if I was able to tell him what I learned now

00:03:17.420 --> 00:03:21.490
on his first movie he would have done something
differently.

00:03:21.490 --> 00:03:29.849
And it’s not like he made a horrific mistake
but he utlimately took his movie off the market

00:03:29.849 --> 00:03:38.770
and there are other ways to do it, no different
from the woman who told me on my last movie

00:03:38.770 --> 00:03:43.310
wasn’t ever exploited on social media.

00:03:43.310 --> 00:03:48.069
The content matter itself should have been
an easy pitch but we didn’t do it.

00:03:48.069 --> 00:03:49.870
So is it a missed opportunity?

00:03:49.870 --> 00:03:54.560
No really because the source material is timeless.

00:03:54.560 --> 00:03:59.500
It’s happening everyday and it still continues
to happen.

00:03:59.500 --> 00:04:03.890
But this movie that I just finished comes
out basically a week before Valentine’s

00:04:03.890 --> 00:04:04.890
Day.

00:04:04.890 --> 00:04:08.180
It’s not accident, it’s a romantic comedy
(I like that idea).

00:04:08.180 --> 00:04:12.200
It helps take the movie to a noticeable level.

00:04:12.200 --> 00:04:15.760
Otherwise you are just going to get in the
mush.

00:04:15.760 --> 00:04:23.949
There’s a great story in the trades about
Netflix buying movies at Sundance last year

00:04:23.949 --> 00:04:25.960
(not this year, last year).

00:04:25.960 --> 00:04:32.909
It’s written by empathetic filmmakers who
said here is a movie that everybody knows

00:04:32.909 --> 00:04:39.559
the title, everybody would like to see it,
it was one Netflix for one week and one the

00:04:39.559 --> 00:04:42.300
first page and then it just disappeared.

00:04:42.300 --> 00:04:50.249
So you have to really know a lot about the
movie, properly spell it and source it.

00:04:50.249 --> 00:04:57.240
I went last night on Amazon to see if my movie
was up for sale because it just came out,

00:04:57.240 --> 00:05:01.629
it required me to go through some closed doors
just to get there.

00:05:01.629 --> 00:05:06.289
I’m talking about searching for it because
when I typed in the name it didn’t pop right

00:05:06.289 --> 00:05:07.289
up.

00:05:07.289 --> 00:05:10.139
And there is a whole other subject, naming
your movie.

00:05:10.139 --> 00:05:16.809
I think distributors would prefer you name
your movie A, B, C, D because it’s closer

00:05:16.809 --> 00:05:19.360
to the beginning of the alphabet.

00:05:19.360 --> 00:05:26.430
Perhaps not now because everybody now is searching
for their films and you just type it in.

00:05:26.430 --> 00:05:29.300
But analytics play a role now.

00:05:29.300 --> 00:05:36.300
You go on to Netflix or on Amazon and you’re
trending with romantic comedies, they’ll

00:05:36.300 --> 00:05:38.290
suggest my movie hopefully.

00:05:38.290 --> 00:05:44.129
So that is a plus to them for making money
and a plus to the distributor who is making

00:05:44.129 --> 00:05:45.129
money.

00:05:45.129 --> 00:05:50.849
But all that stuff never existed, I mean the
format of Netflix and Amazon have changed

00:05:50.849 --> 00:05:53.819
three times in the last month meaning.

00:05:53.819 --> 00:06:00.110
Like right now if you click on a movie on
Amazon a trailer pops up and Netflix, too.

00:06:00.110 --> 00:06:05.379
The trailer for anything you’re watching
(a TV show or whatever), while you’re still

00:06:05.379 --> 00:06:07.759
searching it’s playing a commercial.

00:06:07.759 --> 00:06:14.960
That’s all new innovative technology and
it helps the filmmaker but me to what’s

00:06:14.960 --> 00:06:21.180
really critical is if you know about a filmmaker
like me talking about what I experienced and

00:06:21.180 --> 00:06:23.860
try to avoid that with your own experience.

00:06:23.860 --> 00:06:32.460
So maybe my movie would be called come and
eat the food - C rather than M. Or maybe it

00:06:32.460 --> 00:06:32.939
doesn’t matter.

